+               struct sway_container *_c = floating_container_at(lx, ly, &_surface, &_sx, &_sy);
+               if (_surface && _c && _c != focus && container_is_floating(_c)) {
+                       *surface = _surface;
+                       *sx = _sx;
+                       *sy = _sy;
+                       return _c;
+               }
+
                surface_at_view(focus, lx, ly, surface, sx, sy);
                if (*surface) {
                        return focus;
```

This is what I have so far.  I'm really not sure if this goes into the right direction.  I could not reproduce the steps outlined in #2492 with this code, but I'm not feeling at ease.

I found that the `surface_at_view()` function should be doing what I was thinking about in my comment before. Which is go through all the surfaces within a container including sub-surfaces and if the cursor is within their area.
Right now I'm experimenting with an approach of "raycasting" through the stack of containers.

```c
for (int i = workspace->floating->length - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
  struct sway_container *cn = workspace->floating->items[i];
  surface_at_view(cn, lx, ly, surface, sx, sy);
  if (*surface) return cn;
```
The commit b356b66 shows the basic idea without the popup handling.  I used it for a little bit and it handles really nicely and feels good.
I will test it for a little while.
